.TH TUI_SETTINGDIALOG_INIT 3 2026-11-15 "libtuikit 2.0" "libtuikit Manual"
.SH NAME
tui_settingdialog_init, tui_settingdialog_free, tui_settingdialog_handle,
tui_settingdialog_render \- a dialog that changes one setting
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <tuikit/settingdialog.h>
.PP
.BI "int  tui_settingdialog_init(tui_settingdialog *" d ", const char *" title ,
.BI "                            const char *" text ", const char *" hint ,
.BI "                            char *" buf ", size_t " size ", const char *" initial );
.BI "void tui_settingdialog_free(tui_settingdialog *" d );
.BI "tui_dialog_state tui_settingdialog_handle(tui_settingdialog *" d ", const tui_event *" ev );
.BI "void tui_settingdialog_render(tui_settingdialog *" d ", tui_canvas " c );
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
The dialog of a configuration value: a
.I title
in the frame, the description
.I text
(plain, wrapped, may hold newlines) that says what the value is for, the input
with the current value, a
.I hint
line of the allowed values (may be NULL), and the buttons OK and Cancel.
Like every dialog it neither blocks nor reads the terminal: the program gives it
events and draws it last, and reads
.I state
once it is no longer
.BR TUI_DIALOG_OPEN .
.PP
.BR tui_settingdialog_init ()
copies the texts and edits the value in the caller's
.I buf
(capacity
.IR size ),
starting from
.IR initial .
It returns 0, or \-1 with
.B EINVAL
(no dialog, no buffer) or
.BR ENOMEM .
Set
.I validate
and
.I user
afterwards:
.RS
.nf
int (*validate)(void *user, const char *value, char *err, size_t errsz);
.fi
.RE
A non-zero return refuses the value, with the reason written into
.IR err .
.PP
.BR tui_settingdialog_handle ()
returns the state.
In the input the keys of
.BR tui_lineedit_handle (3)
edit and Enter presses OK.
Tab moves the focus input, OK, Cancel and around; Shift-Tab goes the other way.
On a button Left and Right move between the buttons, Enter presses the focused
one, and Up, Down, PgUp and PgDn scroll a description taller than the box.
Esc and Ctrl-C are Cancel wherever the focus is.
OK runs
.IR validate :
a refusal puts the reason into
.IR error ,
keeps the dialog open and moves the focus back to the input; typing clears the
reason.
Cancel puts the initial value back into the buffer and sets
.BR TUI_DIALOG_CANCELLED ;
OK leaves the value there and sets
.BR TUI_DIALOG_OK .
.PP
.BR tui_settingdialog_render ()
draws the box centred in
.IR c ,
40 to 70 cells wide following the text, a little above the middle, at most the
canvas less a margin of one cell.
The refusal is drawn in the warning role of the scheme, the hint dimmed, the
focused button in the selection role.
On a terminal too small for the field and the buttons it writes one warning line
at the bottom instead.
The cursor is in the input while it has the focus.
.PP
.BR tui_settingdialog_free ()
frees the dialog's copies (not the buffer).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tui_dialog_message (3),
.BR tui_dialog_form_init (3),
.BR tui_lineedit_init (3)
